{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.85,"content_digest":"e3812199406e14ca0f74300119aa66084317563bd465ac397d3ef5bc1b123c00","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:20+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:2454272a7aa9fe869768bdf6","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:interdisciplinary-science:01M0HH7YG3B8YFJSE819XA6R06","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim asserts NP-hardness of SETsparse via a deterministic polynomial-time many-one reduction from 3-SAT. The reduction-completeness meta-principle directly supports this reasoning: an efficient answer-preserving reduction from a known NP-complete problem transfers hardness to the target problem. The reduction type, worst-case complexity scale, and problem-instance variables are compatible.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:20+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.65,"content_digest":"604f83095515a0745792bc0f5ae02ff6229e8d23679801bb6abbe7e5ab860aba","created_at":"2026-08-22T20:44:07+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"85590a9b748af7dd926c9f0379645ee0fe6868a57a7859f84af8407fcad94ba9","input_tokens":5692,"latency_ms":22058,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"e33c24400833c48ba63a3829a16f6e7ac01ff07f46cc8662f258d908f1ab520f","output_tokens":2421,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:6e4bea38a19bd71e8792cb18","meta_principle_id":"meta:socio-technical-systems:socio-technical-joint-optimization","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NKE5CF5A22CAXN8JDSDEJG","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim illustrates a socio-technical gap: the technical explainability subsystem does not supply enough standalone evidence, so the social subsystem (human fallback) absorbs the residual uncertainty. This is consistent with the meta-principle that technical and social subsystems must be designed jointly rather than optimized in isolation.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T20:44:07+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.62,"content_digest":"3deb578c1773b5b28d36cf49868206e03d8710e9b315305cdffe643826b4ecdb","created_at":"2026-08-22T20:24:48+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"404a10c9400ed6ed740059f8af38694acf6ac1d9340fd37c5fb44d271de815c6","input_tokens":5555,"latency_ms":16314,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"0eef137de1f57552964041206bef28a1e486abee3c7f58c7c2098a597704d3c1","output_tokens":2487,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:9536c6f5f01b6786a1fbf59b","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:data-quality-ceiling","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:socio-technical-systems:01M0NJAZAR0BNNXKNAEDYN2DTF","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The quality variance of generated course-aligned examples is an instance of a learning system's output being bounded by the information and biases in its data. This explains why instructor review and curation are needed despite scale benefits, without asserting that the meta-principle alone proves the educational effectiveness claim.","relation_type":"bounded_by","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T20:24:48+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
